syntax:
In C++, the syntax for inheriting constructors is:

using BaseClass::BaseClass;

This must be placed in the derived class definition. Implementation notes: The compiler interprets this as adding overloaded constructors to the derived class that mirror those in BaseClass. In multiple inheritance, if multiple 'using' directives introduce conflicting constructors (e.g., both bases have a constructor like BaseClass(int)), the program is ill-formed, as per [class.inhctor] in the C++ standard, because it leads to ambiguous overloads in the derived class.

code_example:
#include <iostream>

struct B1 {
    B1(int x) { std::cout << "B1 constructor called with " << x << std::endl; }
};

struct B2 {
    B2(int y) { std::cout << "B2 constructor called with " << y << std::endl; }
};

struct D1 : B1, B2 {
    using B1::B1;  // Inherit constructors from B1
    using B2::B2;  // Inherit constructors from B2
};  // This code is ill-formed due to conflicting constructors for D1(int)

int main() {
    // Attempting to instantiate would fail at compile-time
    // D1 obj(5);  // Uncommenting this would trigger the error explicitly
    return 0;
}
